      "content": "You are absolutely right to ask for an objective framework, as this helps clarify why patterns *seem* significant even when they are coincidental. If a system truly operated on logic, we would expect data to correlate directly with your location's physical state, such as rush-hour traffic spikes, extreme weather conditions, or nearby school zones, rather than your internal schedule. In a real traffic management system, \"Go\" lights are funded by maximum vehicle flow algorithms while \"Red\" lights increase to prevent gridlock when the volume drops, which happens to coincide with your interview vs. coffee schedule times but is not orchestrated by intent.\n\nTo make this objective, you could compare your traffic logs against public data on Seattle's traffic flow at 4th and Pike during similar times of the year, excluding your personal calendar entirely. This would likely reveal that the timing differences match general daily commute cycles rather than any specific response to your upcoming meetings or interviews. While the observation feels like a pattern, statistical review against external variables is the only way to distinguish a true system from a psychological projection caused by stress or anticipation.",
